{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Runtime passes. Now let me move to the builder package. I need to:\n1. Add orchestration generation (workflowScript + runs.run).\n2. Update `BuildDoing`/`SaveDoingPrompt` to inject orchestration section.\n3. Remove `check_command`/`check_step_header` SetVariable calls from BuildDoing, BuildEasy, SaveDoingPrompt, SaveEasyPrompt.\n\nLet me first design the orchestration generator. I'll add a new file `internal/builder/orchestration.go` that:\n- `func buildWorkflowOrchestration(doingDir, planDir string) string` — reads tasks.json (or generates from plan), filters success, computes topological order, generates workflowScript string.\n\nActually, let me think about where the orchestration generation logic lives. The task says \"剩余拓扑的极简算法（Kahn/DFS）内联在 builder\". So the topological sort goes in the builder package.\n\nLet me design a minimal task model in builder:\n```go\ntype orchestrationTask struct {\n    ID           string\n    Dependencies []string\n}\n```\n\nFunctions:\n1. `loadOrchestrationTasks(doingDir, planDir string) ([]orchestrationTask, error)`:\n   - Read tasks.json if exists; filter out status=success; for remaining, get dependencies from tasks.json.\n   - If tasks.json missing, scan plan/task*.md, parse \"# 依赖关系\" section for each, all pending.\n\nActually, the workflowScript needs to know the task IDs in topological order (only pending). The dependencies are needed for topological sort. Let me extract dependencies from tasks.json (which has them) or from plan files.\n\nLet me write:\n\n```go\npackage builder\n\nimport (\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"sort\"\n\t\"strings\"\n)\n\ntype orchTask struct {\n\tID           string\n\tDependencies []string\n}\n\n// readOrchTasks returns pending tasks (status != success) with dependencies.\nfunc readOrchTasks(doingDir, planDir string) []orchTask {\n\tif tj, err := loadTasksJSONOrNil(filepath.Join(doingDir, \"tasks.json\")); err == nil {\n\t\tvar out []orchTask\n\t\tfor _, t := range tj.Tasks {\n\t\t\tif t.Status == \"success\" {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tout = append(out, orchTask{ID: t.TaskID, Dependencies: t.Dependencies})\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn out\n\t}\n\t// fallback: scan plan/task*.md\n\treturn scanPlanTasks(planDir)\n}\n```\n\nBut wait — I need a minimal tasks.json reader in builder too. The builder shouldn't import workspace? Actually, builder already imports workspace (`github.com/sunquan/rick/internal/workspace`). So I can use `workspace.LoadTasksJSON`! That's cleaner. But careful about import cycles: builder imports workspace, workspace imports nothing from builder. No cycle. \n\nWait, does workspace import builder? No. So builder → workspace is fine.\n\nActually, let me check: builder already imports `github.com/sunquan/rick/internal/workspace` (yes, in pibuilder.go it imports workspace). Good.\n\nSo I'll use `workspace.LoadTasksJSON` in builder for reading tasks.json.\n\nFor the plan scan (initial draft generation), I need to parse \"# 依赖关系\" from task*.md. Let me write a minimal parser.\n\nThe dependency section format: \n```\n# 依赖关系\ntask1, task2  （无依赖则留空）\n```\nor just \"无\" for no deps.\n\nThe `write_task_md` in the test writes:\n```python\ncontent = (\n    \"# 依赖关系\\n%s\\n\\n\"\n    \"# 任务名称\\n%s\\n\\n\"\n    ...\n) % (deps, name, ...)\n```\nwhere deps = \"无\" or \"task1\".\n\nSo parse: find \"# 依赖关系\" section, read until next \"# \" heading. Split by comma, trim, filter \"无\"/\"无依赖\"/\"none\"/\"\" etc.\n\nLet me write the topological sort (Kahn) for orchTask.\n\nNow, the workflowScript output format. The test needs:\n- literal `workflowScript`\n- literal `runs.run('taskN'` in topological order.\n\nLet me generate a section like:\n\n```\n## pi 编排（workflowScript）\n\n你必须调用 `subagent` 工具，传入以下 `workflowScript`（一次触发，内部按依赖拓扑顺序 `await` 强制顺序执行）：\n\n```javascript\nconst workflowScript = `\nconst r_task1 = await runs.run('task1', {agent:'worker', task:'请按 <path> 中的 task1.md 执行'});\nconst r_task2 = await runs.run('task2', {agent:'worker', task:'...'});\nreturn {task1: r_task1.output, task2: r_task2.output};\n`;\n```\n```\n\nActually, the regex just needs `runs.run('task1'` and `runs.run('task2'`. Let me generate lines like:\n\n```\nconst task1 = await runs.run('task1', {agent:'worker', task:'执行 task1'});\n```\n\nWait, but for topological order: task1 before task2. Since we `await` sequentially, the order in the code is the order of execution. For task3 with no dependency, it can be anywhere but the test just checks task1 before task2 and presence of all 3. So a simple linear order works (topological sort produces a valid linear order).\n\nActually, for a proper workflowScript, dependencies should be respected via await. A simple linear order (topological) with sequential await is correct. Let me do that.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: the test for job_2 (task1 success, task2 pending deps on task1, task3 pending no deps) expects sequence contains task2 and task3 but NOT task1. And no ordering requirement between task2/task3 (well, only that task1 not present). So filtering success then topological sort of remaining is fine. But task2 depends on task1 which is now \"completed\" — so task2's dependency is satisfied (task1 already done). The remaining topology for {task2 (dep task1), task3 (no dep)}: task2 depends on task1 (not in set), so task2 has in-degree 0 in the remaining graph (since task1 is removed). So topological sort gives [task2, task3] or [task3, task2]. Either works.\n\nOK, let me write the orchestration generator.\n\nAlso, the task KR1 says \"首轮 tasks.json 不存在时由 builder 扫描 `plan/task*.md`，用极简字符串提取「# 依赖关系」章节生成初稿：task 列表 + 依赖 + status=pending\". So when tasks.json doesn't exist, the builder should ALSO write the initial tasks.json draft. But for dry-run (BuildDoing), we probably shouldn't write files. Hmm. Let me think.\n\nActually, the test for job_1 (no tasks.json) runs `rick doing job_1 --dry-run`. This goes through `handler.DoingDryRun` → `builder.SaveDoingPrompt`. If SaveDoingPrompt writes tasks.json, that would be a side effect in dry-run. But the test fixture is in a temp dir and gets cleaned up, so writing tasks.json there is harmless. But it's cleaner to not write in dry-run.\n\nActually wait, let me look at the current DoingDryRun flow again:\n```go\nfunc DoingDryRun(jobID string) error {\n    ...\n    tasks, err := loadTasksFromPlan(planDir)  // uses parser!\n    ...\n    task := tasks[0]\n    if tasksJSON, err := executor.LoadTasksJSON(tasksJSONPath); err == nil {\n        for _, t := range tasks {\n            if status, err := tasksJSON.GetTaskStatus(t.ID); err != nil || status != \"success\" {\n                task = t\n                break\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    promptFile, _, _, err := builder.NewPIBuilder().SaveDoingPrompt(task.ID, doingDir, planDir, rickDir, jobID, \"doing_check\")\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nI need to rewrite `DoingDryRun` too. And `loadTasksFromPlan` uses parser (to be removed).\n\nThe new `DoingDryRun` should:\n- Call `builder.NewPIBuilder().SaveDoingPrompt(...)` with the orchestration.\n- The orchestration generation reads tasks.json (or plan) internally.\n\nSo I'll change `SaveDoingPrompt` to not require a specific `task.ID` for orchestration — actually, the current `SaveDoingPrompt(taskID, ...)` generates a per-task prompt. But now the doing prompt is a job-level orchestration prompt (parent orchestrator), not a per-task prompt.\n\nLet me reconsider. The new doing prompt is a single prompt (the parent orchestrator) that contains the workflowScript. So `SaveDoingPrompt` should generate ONE prompt for the whole job (with orchestration section), not per-task.\n\nSo I'll change the signature of `SaveDoingPrompt` and `BuildDoing`. Let me design:\n\n`BuildDoing(params)` — the params include rick_dir, plan_dir, doing_dir, job_id. It generates the full doing instance with orchestration. No per-task ID needed.\n\n`SaveDoingPrompt(params)` or `SaveDoingPrompt(doingDir, planDir, rickDir, jobID)`.\n\nBut wait, there are other callers of SaveDoingPrompt. Let me check: `handler/doing.go` (DoingDryRun) is the only caller of SaveDoingPrompt. And BuildDoing is called by pibuilder.BuildPrompt (doing case). Let me check who calls BuildPrompt/BuildDoing... Actually, BuildDoing is called via `BuildPrompt` which is part of RuntimeBuilder interface. Let me check if anything calls BuildPrompt(\"doing\", ...).\n\nLet me grep for SaveDoingPrompt and BuildDoing usage."}}
