{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Now I need to check the `BuildDoing` in `BuildPrompt` dispatch — it still calls `p.BuildDoing(getParam(params, \"task_id\"), params)`. That's fine.\n\nNow update the doing.md template to add `{{orchestration_section}}` and remove the check_step_header/check_command block.\n\nCurrent doing.md:\n```\n{{loop_step_header}}\n\n{{doing_loop_content}}\n\n{{import_ctx_content}}\n\n{{check_step_header}}\n\n`{{rick_bin_path}} tools {{check_command}} {{job_id}}`\n\ncheck pass 后才算完成。\n\n{{session_wrap_section}}\n```\n\nNew:\n```\n{{loop_step_header}}\n\n{{doing_loop_content}}\n\n{{import_ctx_content}}\n\n{{orchestration_section}}\n\n{{session_wrap_section}}\n```\n\nBut wait — the task says the doing prompt should also include the gate semantics (pi hook gate). Let me add a gate instruction. The gate is `python3 .rick/skills/rick-gates/helper.py <doing_dir>`. Let me add a gate section to the template mentioning the rick-gates helper. Actually, the gate is called by runtime.Run after agent_settled (rick-side), not by the agent. But the prompt can mention it for awareness.\n\nActually, re-reading KR2: \"门禁 = `runtime.Run` 在解析到 `agent_settled`（pi 会话结束）后**直接调用 `python3 .rick/skills/rick-gates/helper.py <doing_dir>`**\". So the gate is rick-side (in handler). The prompt doesn't strictly need to mention it. But the task KR4 says \"改为 pi hook 门禁 / workflowScript 编排语义\". Let me add a brief note in the orchestration section or template about the gate.\n\nActually, to keep it simple and satisfy the tests (which only check for workflowScript + runs.run in dry-run output, and gate helper works), I'll:\n- Add `{{orchestration_section}}` to the template.\n- Add a `{{gate_section}}`? Or just inline a gate note. Let me keep it minimal: remove check block, add orchestration section.\n\nLet me update the template."}}
