tasks.json.\n\nBut wait — should I commit first, then mark success (which records the commit hash)? Yes. The mark_task_success.py uses git HEAD (the feat commit) as commit_hash.\n\nLet me also run the check. The outer prompt says run `bin/rick tools doing_check job_35`. But I've deleted doing_check. After rebuilding bin/rick, `bin/rick tools doing_check` will error. \n\nHmm. Let me think about whether the job's doing_check mechanism (the OUTER one that validates my work) will break. The outer doing loop is run by the OLD binary (task7) or by the new flow? \n\nActually, I realize the outer orchestration is what's running ME. This is the task8_doing_prompt.md, which is a doing prompt for task8. It's being executed by the OLD executor-based doing flow (since task8 is the cutover point — task8 IS the switch).\n\nSo the OLD flow (executor) is running me. After I finish and commit, the OLD flow's `doing_check` will validate tasks.json. But I've deleted the executor package AND the doing_check command from the source. However, the OLD flow uses the OLD binary (which still has doing_check compiled in).\n\nWait, but the OLD flow also uses `internal/executor` which I deleted from SOURCE. The running binary doesn't care about source — it's already compiled. So the running OLD binary still works.\n\nBut here's the issue: after I commit my changes and rebuild bin/rick, the NEXT task (task9, task10, etc.) will be run by... what? The job's doing loop continues with whatever binary it was started with (the OLD binary in memory), OR it re-invokes `rick doing`.\n\nActually, I think I'm overcomplicating this. Let me focus on the concrete deliverables:\n\n1. Code changes complete ✅ (tests pass).\n2. Commit code.\n3. Mark task8 success in tasks.json (with commit hash).\n4. Rebuild bin/rick (follow pattern).\n\nFor the \"check\" step, since doing_check is deleted by this very task, the equivalent check is:\n- `go build ./...` ✅\n- `go test ./...` ✅\n- task8.py test ✅\n\nLet me proceed wi