{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"The new binary correctly shows only learning_check and dream_check (no doing_check/plan_check/easy_check). The rebuild is correct.\n\nNow let me mark task8 success. I'll use mark_task_success.py which reads HEAD commit hash and updates tasks.json. But wait — I want the commit_hash to be the \"feat(task8)\" commit, not the \"rebuild bin/rick\" commit. Actually, looking at previous tasks, the commit_hash in tasks.json points to the feat commit (e.g., task7's commit_hash was f4f5f03 which is \"rebuild bin/rick (final)\", but also earlier af47384 which was \"fix(task7)\").\n\nActually, looking more carefully at the final tasks.json, task7's commit_hash = f4f5f0338f2d33ca66b318ff773793fa2238fdf8 = f4f5f03 \"chore(task7): rebuild bin/rick (final)\". So the commit_hash points to the LATEST commit for that task (the rebuild).\n\nHmm, actually it doesn't matter much — the commit_hash just needs to be non-empty for the gate. The convention is it points to the task's code commit.\n\nLet me think: the mark_task_success.py uses `git rev-parse HEAD` = current HEAD. After my \"rebuild bin/rick\" commit, HEAD = 3f66f44. If I run mark_task_success.py now, it'll set commit_hash = 3f66f44 (the rebuild commit).\n\nBut conventionally, I want the commit_hash to represent the task's code. Previous tasks seem to have commit_hash = the final commit (including rebuild). Let me check task9's pattern: task9 commit_hash = ab9e476f11556f669f68f01e10d4c968842240ca = ab9e476 \"chore(task9): mark task9 success in tasks.json\". Wait, that's the \"mark success\" commit itself?\n\nLet me look at the tasks.json committed version for task9's commit_hash. From the git log, ab9e476 is \"chore(task9): mark task9 success in tasks.json\". So task9's commit_hash points to its own \"mark success\" commit? That's odd (circular), but it's what the flow produced — the mark_task_success.py ran, got HEAD (which was t